ou  have any fear for your life any fear no  not in a real sense I try to live life  without looking over my shoulder so to  speak because that would be very  frustrating and you growing up I talk  about this in the book that we were  always under the threat of danger there  were I remember in school there were  kidnap threats levied on my family and  we had teachers going to school police  officers posing as teachers and and that  type of thing and I kind of joked about  the fact that my brother and I when he  got his license we were going driving  around somewhere as teenagers do and we  had this cop car fall this around it was  like cramping our style you know we  couldn't be kids so you know but that  was that was just kind of you know  danger was the constant companion but  you you don't at least we didn't live  our lives that way we were always  encouraged to just live a normal life  yes mom  deal with the weight of the legacy of  her husband I'm sorry how have I helped  her no have you I was observed her deal  with it well I think she's done very  well I mean and I expressed this in the  book how is saddened me or really  brought tears to my eyes to see that  when she and my father met and  subsequently got married they were in  New England or  Boston at the time and they had a lot of  privileges that they didn't have coming  back to the South since she was an  artist when she met my father she was in  the conservatory New England  Conservatory studying music concert  singer so they had to make a choice do  we go back to the south and give up some  of the privileges just simply going to  the Opera to ballet or any of the art  types events without having the  opportunity like in the South you knew  you would be sitting in the balcony or  you would have to enter from the rear  these sound like very small things  particularly to kids today who have no  real sense of of that era so it brought  tears to my eyes to for them both in  their early 20s I mean think of 